// The spindle assembly checkpoint — attachment as a sight line.
//
// Before a cell divides, every chromosome must be captured by microtubules
// from BOTH spindle poles (bi-orientation): each sister chromatid's
// kinetochore faces, and attaches to, the pole it will travel to. The
// checkpoint HALTS the cell until the last kinetochore is attached — one
// unattached chromosome and anaphase does not fire.
//
// Here "attached to a pole" = "the pole has a clear line to the kinetochore".
// Occlusion is the one fact logic cannot invent: whether a pole can reach a
// kinetochore depends on where every other chromosome actually is. The 3D
// emits that ground truth as visible/4 (sees() published as data, for set
// members); rules.pl does the quantifying — "no kinetochore is unattached":
//     all_visible(Pole, Set, T) :- \+ (set_member(Set, K), \+ visible_at(Pole, K, T)).
// Code and Prolog are cool, but they don't know 3D — so the 3D is the
// compiler that pushes back: you cannot script anaphase for a cell whose
// chromosomes aren't really bi-oriented.

theme clay
view iso

time metaphase_early 1   // one kinetochore still unattached — the cell waits
time metaphase_late 6    // the last chromosome has congressed — cleared to divide

// the two spindle poles, on the spindle axis (x). Each sister travels to,
// and must be seen by, the pole its colour matches.
sphere pole_l r(0.4) at(-6 1.5 0) color(#c8451f)
sphere pole_r r(0.4) at( 6 1.5 0) color(#1f7a6d)
set poles pole_l pole_r

// three chromosomes already congressed on the metaphase plate (x=0),
// sisters split left/right, each kinetochore facing its pole
box chr1_l size(0.4 1.1 0.4) at(-0.35 1.5 -2.4) color(#c8451f)
box chr1_r size(0.4 1.1 0.4) at( 0.35 1.5 -2.4) color(#1f7a6d)
box chr2_l size(0.4 1.1 0.4) at(-0.35 1.5 -0.8) color(#c8451f)
box chr2_r size(0.4 1.1 0.4) at( 0.35 1.5 -0.8) color(#1f7a6d)
box chr3_l size(0.4 1.1 0.4) at(-0.35 1.5  0.8) color(#c8451f)
box chr3_r size(0.4 1.1 0.4) at( 0.35 1.5  0.8) color(#1f7a6d)

// the lagging chromosome: not yet congressed. Its left kinetochore sits
// behind its own sister from pole_l's view — mono-oriented, unattached to
// the left pole. This is the one that holds the checkpoint.
box chr4_l size(0.4 1.1 0.4) at( 1.6 1.5 2.4) color(#c8451f)
box chr4_r size(0.4 1.1 0.4) at( 0.9 1.5 2.4) color(#1f7a6d)

set left_kt  chr1_l chr2_l chr3_l chr4_l
set right_kt chr1_r chr2_r chr3_r chr4_r

// congression: the lagging chromosome slides onto the plate, swinging its
// left kinetochore into the left pole's line of sight — attachment made.
move chr4_l to(-0.35 1.5 2.4) start(2) over(2) ease(in-out)
move chr4_r to( 0.35 1.5 2.4) start(2) over(2) ease(in-out)

// anaphase: only now, checkpoint satisfied, sisters are drawn to their poles.
// The kinetochore fibres appear at anaphase (so they can't obstruct the
// metaphase audit) and shorten as each sister is reeled in — anaphase A.
move chr1_l to(-5 1.5 -2.4) start(7) over(2) ease(in-out)
move chr1_r to( 5 1.5 -2.4) start(7) over(2) ease(in-out)
move chr2_l to(-5 1.5 -0.8) start(7) over(2) ease(in-out)
move chr2_r to( 5 1.5 -0.8) start(7) over(2) ease(in-out)
move chr3_l to(-5 1.5  0.8) start(7) over(2) ease(in-out)
move chr3_r to( 5 1.5  0.8) start(7) over(2) ease(in-out)
move chr4_l to(-5 1.5  2.4) start(7) over(2) ease(in-out)
move chr4_r to( 5 1.5  2.4) start(7) over(2) ease(in-out)

link fibre_1l between(pole_l chr1_l) r(0.03) color(#e0a08c) appear(7)
link fibre_1r between(pole_r chr1_r) r(0.03) color(#8cc4bb) appear(7)
link fibre_2l between(pole_l chr2_l) r(0.03) color(#e0a08c) appear(7)
link fibre_2r between(pole_r chr2_r) r(0.03) color(#8cc4bb) appear(7)
link fibre_3l between(pole_l chr3_l) r(0.03) color(#e0a08c) appear(7)
link fibre_3r between(pole_r chr3_r) r(0.03) color(#8cc4bb) appear(7)
link fibre_4l between(pole_l chr4_l) r(0.03) color(#e0a08c) appear(7)
link fibre_4r between(pole_r chr4_r) r(0.03) color(#8cc4bb) appear(7)

// --- the checkpoint, in language ------------------------------------------
? when sees(pole_l chr4_l) during(0 6)          // unattached, then attached
? blocked-by(pole_l chr4_l) at(metaphase_early) // what hides it: its own sister
check never sees(pole_l chr4_l) during(0 1.9)   // the lag is real...
check sees(pole_l chr4_l) at(metaphase_late)    // ...and congression attached it
check always sees(pole_l chr1_l) during(0 6)    // a proper attachment holds throughout

// --- the checkpoint, derived ----------------------------------------------
// left pole must see every left kinetochore, right pole every right one.
// Early it fails and unseen/4 names the culprit; late, both poles are clear.
?- all_visible(pole_l, left_kt, metaphase_early)   // fails — a kinetochore is unattached
?- unseen(pole_l, left_kt, metaphase_early, C)      // C = chr4_l, the lagging one
?- all_visible(pole_r, right_kt, metaphase_early)  // the right pole is fully attached
?- all_visible(pole_l, left_kt, metaphase_late)    // cleared — the cell may divide
